From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 16:43:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Keep string keys and names as UTF-16 code units

A JavaScript string is a sequence of UTF-16 code units and may contain
unpaired surrogates, and IndexedDB string keys and the names of
databases, object stores and indexes are such strings: string keys
compare by code unit (compare two keys) and name lists sort by code
unit. Servo turned them into Rust Strings, replacing each unpaired
surrogate with U+FFFD, so "\uD800" and "\uDC00" were the same key, a
key read back differed from the one stored, and indexedDB.cmp() called
them equal.

Utf16String (storage_traits) holds the code units and orders by them.
It is the type of IndexedDBKeyType::String and of every database, store
and index name in the protocol, the manager (IndexedDBDescription), the
engine and the tables, and of the names script's IndexedDB objects keep
(IDBDatabase, IDBObjectStore and its index set, IDBIndex, IDBFactory's
maps). Script reads a string key's code units from the JS string and
makes JS strings from code units, so keys are exact end to end. The
order-preserving key encoding, Gecko's, already encoded UTF-16 code
units; it now takes them directly, and decoding no longer rejects
unpaired surrogates. Encoded keys of well-formed strings are unchanged.

Names are stored as BLOBs of their code units in big-endian order
(Utf16String::to_be_bytes), as text can't hold unpaired surrogates, in
the database, object_store and object_store_index tables. That changes
the schema: SCHEMA_VERSION is 2, and databases stored with version 1
are deleted and created anew when opened (owner rule, no migration).
The client storage registry keeps a well-formed database name as the
text it was, so existing databases keep their entries, and a name with
an unpaired surrogate as a BLOB of its code units, which SQLite never
finds equal to text; both registry backends do this. Database
directories are named by UUID, so names never reach a path.

Names still enter and leave script as WebIDL DOMStrings, which Servo's
bindings convert with U+FFFD replacement, so a name given by a page
still loses its unpaired surrogates, and the six WPT subtests that
rename a store to or create stores with such names stay expected FAIL.
Only name_from_dom_string and dom_string_from_name in indexeddb/key.rs
cross that boundary.
IndexedDB sorts name lists itself (sorted_name_list), so
DOMStringList::new_sorted, which only IndexedDB used, is gone.
IDBTransaction.objectStore() checks its scope by store id instead of
by name.

Tests: on every executor, store and index names with unpaired
surrogates, distinct from their U+FFFD forms, through create, rename,
the metadata and a reopen, a database name stored as its code units,
and string keys with unpaired surrogates stored, read in code unit
order and selected by a key range; the encoding round-trips and sorts
them. The registry keeps such database names apart and stores
well-formed ones as text (host SQLite test inspecting the registry,
and an RDB test for the device). A fork WPT test,
tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/string-keys-code-units.any.js,
covers keys, ranges, cursors, in-line keys and cmp().

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 112 passed, 1 skipped (P2.2: 110). WPT IndexedDB/ (4,511 subtests), 8 processes, host SpiderMonkey: 3,287 pass, as P2.2, and all 837 tests including /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ as expected; the new fork test passes 16 of 16 subtests in its four globals, and the P2.1 binary fails all 16. `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`: no warning; `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both: no warning; `./mach test-tidy`: only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory. DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage`: RDB only 113 passed, 3 skipped (P2.2: 111), SQLite and RDB 195 passed, 4 skipped (P2.2: 192), the new RDB registry and backend tests included; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ on the DAYU200 (QuickJS and RDB): 98 of 98.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent designed and wrote the change and the tests, and ran the host builds and test runs)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
